page=notes sect=7 summary="notes, exit strings, signals and faults"
    src=lib/vx-note/note.c
    keys=note,notes,notify,noted,interrupt,signal,signals,exit,status,fault,trap,crash,notepg,noteid,sigint

# DESCRIPTION

A note is Plan 9's way to interrupt a process: a string of at most 128 bytes, `VX_ERRMAX`, posted to it from outside, such as `interrupt` when the user types `^C` or `posix: SIGTERM pid=12` from kill(1).
A fault is one too: a process that reads address 16 gets the note `sys: trap: fault read addr=0x10 pc=0x2186cc`.
A process that has asked for its notes has each handed to a function of its own, which deals with it and goes on, or declines it; a process that has not, or that declines, ends, and the note is its exit string (ADR-0010).

A process ends with an exit string too: empty for success, anything else the reason, in the same 128 bytes.
A native program's `vx_main` returns it, as Plan 9's `exits` takes it; a killed one has the killer's words, and one ended by a fault the fault's.
A parent reads it from its child's wait record, rc(1) keeps it in `$status`, and svcd(8) prints it when a service exits.

So one string carries the whole story of how a process was interrupted and how it ended, in words a person reads.
This page describes how notes are posted, handled and grouped, how POSIX's signals are built on them, and where a fault goes that no one handles.

@node=posting title="Posting a note"

The kernel's call is `thread_interrupt`, given a task handle with `MANAGE`, a thread or 0 for any thread, and the note (thread(2)).
A call the thread is blocked in returns `VX_ERR_INTERRUPTED`, and on its way back to user mode the thread is diverted to its task's handler with the note.
Up to eight notes wait for a thread, delivered oldest first; a ninth is refused with `VX_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT`.

A process rarely makes the call itself.
procfs(4) holds every process's task, and posts a note for whoever writes it to `/proc/<pid>/note`, or to every process of a note group through `/proc/<pid>/notepg` ({note groups|#groups}).
These post notes now:

- kill(1), and C's `kill`, write a signal's note to `note` or `notepg` ({signals|#signals});
- ptyd(4) writes `interrupt` for `^C`, and the notes of `^Z` and `^\`, to the `notepg` of its terminal's foreground group, and ends the reads it holds so the note is seen at once;
- procfs sends `posix: SIGCHLD pid=<pid>` to a process that has asked, with `childnotes`, to hear of its children;
- anyone may write a note to `/proc/<pid>/note` with echo(1).

@node=handling title="Handling notes"

A native program asks for its notes with vx-rt's `vx_notify` (`lib/vx-rt/note.c`), Plan 9's `notify` in standard C.
It binds the task's in-task exception handler (exception(2)), and from then on each note is handed to the program's function with the `vx_exception` it came in:

```c
typedef vx_noted vx_note_handler(vx_exception *e, vx_str note);
```

The function answers `VX_NCONT`, to go on where the thread was diverted, with the registers in the exception, perhaps changed; or `VX_NDFLT`, to end the program with the note as its exit string.
It runs on the thread's own stack, below where the thread was; its floating-point registers are saved around it.
`vx_notify(nullptr)` goes back to ending at the first note.

A program that has not called `vx_notify` has no handler, and the kernel ends it at its first note, with the note as its exit string, as a Plan 9 process that has not called `notify` is killed.

A fault reaches the same function, as a note in Plan 9's words, which `lib/vx-note/note.c` makes from the exception for the kernel and vx-rt alike:

| Exception | Note |
| page fault | `sys: trap: fault read addr=0x10 pc=0x401000`, or `write`, `exec` |
| illegal instruction | `sys: trap: illegal instruction pc=...` |
| arithmetic | `sys: trap: arithmetic pc=...` |
| alignment | `sys: trap: misaligned addr=... pc=...` |
| FP disabled | `sys: trap: fp disabled pc=...` |
| breakpoint | `sys: breakpoint pc=...` |
| a pager's page not in time | `sys: trap: page not supplied addr=... pc=...` |
| any other | `sys: trap: general fault pc=...` |

A fault the function declines is not ended there: vx-rt takes the handler away and runs the instruction again, so that it faults with nothing in the task to catch it and goes on to whoever is bound after ({faults|#exceptions}).

@node=groups title="Note groups"

Note groups and sessions are procfs's, not the kernel's: the kernel posts a note to one task, and procfs knows which tasks a group holds.
A process is in its parent's group and session unless whoever spawned it registered it with a group of its own (`PROC_NOTEG`) or a session and group of its own (`PROC_SETSID`).
`/proc/<pid>/noteid` reads a process's group, and a write moves it to another group of its session, or to a new one named by its own pid; `ctl`'s `setsid` gives it a session of its own.

A note written to `notepg` goes to every member of the group, and, unlike 9front's, to the writer too if it is one, before the write returns.

svcd starts each service in a session and group of its own, as Plan 9's daemons run, so a note to one group never reaches the rest of the system.
svcd itself takes no note: it has no handler, and a note would end it, and the system with it.

POSIX's process group is the note group: `setpgid` writes `noteid`, `setsid` writes `ctl`, and a terminal's foreground group is the group ptyd writes its notes to (posix(7)).

@node=signals title="Signals"

POSIX's signals are notes, as 9front's APE builds them (ADR-0010).
`kill` writes the signal's note to `/proc/<pid>/note`, or to `notepg` for a group: Plan 9's word where Plan 9 has one, and otherwise `posix: ` and the signal's name, with the sender's pid.
The C library's note handler (`ports/musl/vx/signal.c`) maps each note back to its signal through one table, `lib/vx-posix/posix.h`:

| Note | Signal |
| `hangup` | `SIGHUP` |
| `interrupt` | `SIGINT` |
| `alarm` | `SIGALRM` |
| `sys: write on closed pipe` | `SIGPIPE` |
| `killed` | `SIGKILL` |
| `sys: trap: fault ...`, `sys: trap: general fault` | `SIGSEGV` |
| `sys: trap: illegal instruction`, `sys: trap: fp disabled` | `SIGILL` |
| `sys: trap: arithmetic` | `SIGFPE` |
| `sys: trap: misaligned`, `sys: trap: page not supplied` | `SIGBUS` |
| `sys: breakpoint`, `sys: trap: step` | `SIGTRAP` |
| `posix: SIGTERM pid=12` | the signal named, sent by process 12 |

Dispositions, the mask and the pending set live in the process, in the C library, as they do in APE.
A signal that comes while the program runs its own code is delivered at once, on its stack; one that comes inside the library is made pending, the call it interrupted returns, and the handler runs on the way out, after which the call returns `EINTR` or, for `SA_RESTART`, is made again.
A note that is no signal ends the process with the note, as in Plan 9.
A fault becomes `SIGSEGV`, `SIGBUS`, `SIGILL`, `SIGFPE` or `SIGTRAP`, with its address; a fault that is blocked or ignored, which would only happen again, ends the process.

What a process cannot do to itself, procfs does: a note naming `SIGKILL` kills the process with `killed`; one naming `SIGSTOP` stops it; one naming `SIGCONT` continues it and is then delivered, for its handler.
Stopping is not a note (ADR-0011): a stop suspends every thread (`thread_suspend`, thread(2)), and a stopping signal's default writes `stop <sig>` to the process's own `ctl`, so its parent's wait learns which signal it was.

An exit string becomes a wait status as APE's does: the empty string is 0, a decimal number is that exit status, a string the table knows is death by that signal, and anything else is exit status 1.
A child started by `posix_spawn` or `exec` keeps its parent's mask and ignored signals, carried in its spawn message.

@node=exceptions title="Faults and the debugger's stops"

A fault in user mode is an exception, and goes to whoever is bound to take it, in this order (exception(2)):

- a debugger's port, bound first-chance, which sees the fault before the program does;
- the task's in-task handler, which vx-rt's `vx_notify` binds, and which hands the fault to the program as a note;
- the task's exception port;
- the kernel's default, which ends the task with the fault's note as its exit string.

procfs binds itself in two of those places.
For every process it binds the exception port, last in line, to catch the faults nothing else takes ({crash directories|#crash}).
When a debugger sets a breakpoint or a watchpoint, or opens `/proc/<pid>/events`, procfs binds the first-chance port too, and a thread that faults, or reaches a breakpoint, stops there with an event until the debugger lets it go, which passes a fault on to the program (procfs(4)#debug).
dbg(1) does all of this through those files.

A note is an exception only to the in-task handler: it never stops at a debugger's port, and a process being debugged gets its notes as it otherwise would.

@node=crash title="Crash directories"

A fault that no debugger and no handler takes reaches procfs.
procfs stops the process's other threads and saves it as a directory shaped like `/proc/<pid>`, `/tmp/crash/<name>.<pid>`: its status, mappings, images and threads' registers as they were, its writable memory, and a file `note` holding the fault in Plan 9's words.
It prints a line on the console, then kills the process with the fault's note as its exit string, as any unhandled fault ends it.

dbg(1) opens a crash directory as it opens a live process, so `bt` and `print` show the program as it died.
A POSIX program whose fault takes the default goes the same way: the C library reports the fatal signal, then hands the fault on to procfs.

@node=rc title="rc and notes"

rc(1) asks for its notes, and runs a function of its own for each it knows, before its next instruction: `sighup` for `hangup`, `sigint` for `interrupt`, `sigquit` for `quit`, `sigalrm` for `alarm`, `sigkill` for `kill`, `sigfpe` for `sys: fp:`, `sigterm` for `term`, and for each the VectraOS note of the same signal: `sigfpe` for `sys: trap: arithmetic`, `sigterm` for `posix: SIGTERM pid=`<n> from kill(1), `sigquit` for `posix: SIGQUIT` from `^\` (rc(1)#notes).
Without its function, an interrupt or quit stops what rc is doing and goes back to reading commands if it is interactive, and ends it if not; any other note ends it.

rc's `$status` is each command's wait message, its name and pid before its exit string, as 9front's rc has it; from rc's test, a script that exits with `2`:

```rc
% echo 'for(a in $*) echo arg $a' > /tmp/t.rc; echo 'exit $#*' >> /tmp/t.rc
% rc /tmp/t.rc x 'y z'; if(~ $status 'rc '*': 2') echo status 2
arg x
arg y z
status 2
```

# EXAMPLES

A POSIX child that reads address 16 with `SIGSEGV` at its default, and its parent's wait, from `tests/posix/ctest.c`:

```c
CHECK(spawn_child("segv", nullptr, &child) == 0);
CHECK(waitpid(child, &status, 0) == child && WIFSIGNALED(status) &&
      WTERMSIG(status) == SIGSEGV);
```

The child's end on the console: the C library's report,

```text
vx-musl: ctest (pid 34): fatal signal 11 at pc 0x2186cc, address 0x10
```

then procfs's, as it saves the crash directory and ends the child with the fault's note:

`procfs: ctest.34: sys: trap: fault read addr=0x10 pc=0x2186cc; saved in /tmp/crash`

Its exit string, `sys: trap: fault read addr=0x10 pc=0x2186cc`, is what the table maps to `SIGSEGV` for `waitpid`, and the test then reads the same words from `/tmp/crash/ctest.34/note`.

# FILES

: `/proc/<pid>/note`, `notepg`, `noteid`
  A process's notes, its group's, and its group.
: `/tmp/crash/<name>.<pid>/`
  A crash directory.

# SEE ALSO

thread(2), exception(2), task(2), procfs(4), ptyd(4), dbg(1), rc(1), kill(1), ps(1), svcd(8), posix(7), handles(7); ADR-0010, ADR-0011 and `docs/05-debugger.md` §5 in the repository

# BUGS

A note does not interrupt a 9P call: a process blocked in a file read, or on the console, sees its note only when the call is done, unless the server ends the read, as ptyd and procfs's `wait` do.
The client's `Tflush`, which ADR-0010 has a note send, comes with the pipelined 9P client in M6 step 6d.
The console driver drops `^C` (drv-uart-16550(3)), so at the console it interrupts nothing.

The design queues notes per task; the kernel queues them per thread, so a note to any thread goes to the one chosen when it is posted.
A full exception port, as a debugger that does not read its events leaves, kills the task instead of stopping it (exception(2)).

Signals lack an alternate signal stack, the registers in a handler's `ucontext`, `sigqueue` and real-time queueing; per-thread masks and `sigaltstack` come with threads in M6 step 6d.
Nothing posts `alarm`: the C library has no `alarm`, `setitimer` or timers (time(7)).

Anyone who can open `/proc/<pid>` can post it a note, or a note to its group, until capability tokens (M10).
Crash directories are lost at reboot, never pruned, and keep secrets: memory marked `NODUMP` is not kept out, nor is anything encrypted.
There are no thread, image or exit events for a debugger (M14).
